<div class="card"><div class="sub-title" style="margin-top:0"><span class="a-tag" id="a1">A1</span>Methodology &amp; Sources</div>
<div class="prose">
<p>This review applies the D7 Governance &amp; Forensic Accounting (GFA) framework — a six-pillar forensic model operationalised for D7 due diligence — adapted for an Indian listed issuer. The composite is the higher of three independent readings: <strong>max(weighted blend, Pillar-5 floor, override floor)</strong>. Pillar 5 (forensic accounting) carries the dominant weight; an override sets a floor, never a ceiling, and forces human review.</p>
<p><strong>India adaptations applied:</strong> the auditor’s report &amp; <strong>CARO 2020</strong> replace the 10-K/ICFR spine (here the CARO is clean — no fraud reported — which is disconfirming evidence, recorded as such); a <strong>standalone-versus-consolidated reconciliation</strong> lens is used to surface subsidiary drag and intra-group flows; the <strong>Dechow F-Score is treated as directional only</strong>, as it is calibrated on US SEC (AAER) enforcement with no Indian equivalent — Indian forums (SEBI adjudication, NCLT/NCLAT, SAT) are the local analogue; litigation is read from Indian forums (no PACER). The <strong>generic revenue-integrity assessment</strong> is applied (not the retail protocol), centred on whether related-party transactions affect revenue, profit or cash flow.</p>
<p><strong>Sources:</strong> Zee Entertainment FY2024-25 Annual Report (Ind AS standalone &amp; consolidated, auditor’s report, CARO, Key Audit Matters, Note 46 related-party disclosures, segment Note 40); D7 CDD report FORT10070 (§5 screening, §6 adverse media, §7 regulatory, §8 litigation), data fetched 19 May 2026. The audited annual filing is the authoritative source for financial-statement data; secondary sources do not override it. Peer benchmarking is not applied in this review.</p>
