<div class="prose">
<p><strong>Content inventory &amp; NRV (auditor KAM).</strong> The one genuine accounting-judgement area is the <strong>₹6,412 Cr (standalone) content/programming inventory</strong>, amortised on management’s estimate of future revenue potential, with a <strong>₹336.8 Cr net-realisable-value write-down</strong> recognised. The auditor flagged <strong>two</strong> Key Audit Matters bearing on asset recoverability: recoverability of content advances and inventory valuation, and separately the recoverability of investment in subsidiaries carried at cost — the latter a direct related-party signal carried in P3. This is a real estimate-sensitivity (changing viewing patterns, content performance), driving the long inventory days (DIO ~530), and is recorded as a corroborated <strong>watch</strong> — not a misstatement, as the auditor was satisfied with the provision.</p>
<p><strong>Manipulation-pattern map (Schilit).</strong> Of the patterns tested, the revenue families (recording revenue too soon / bogus revenue / channel-stuffing) are <strong>clear</strong> — contradicted by declining receivables and clean DSRI. The live pattern is <strong>related-party / round-tripping risk</strong> (the FD/ICD/affiliate flows), assessed in P3 as a finding on value-leakage but <em>not</em> rising to confirmed misstatement (no auditor exception, clean CARO). Cookie-jar / smoothing: not evidenced — exceptional charges moderated transparently (FY23 ₹667 Cr → FY25 ₹106 Cr) and were disclosed.</p>
