<div class="callout amber" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>RPT verdict on the revenue/profit/cash question.</strong> Related parties have <strong>limited effect on reported revenue</strong> (intra-group subscription/content flows are ordinary and modest relative to ₹7,712 Cr group revenue), but a <strong>material effect on profit and cash</strong>: the ₹93.1 Cr ATL Media dividend supports standalone profit and the affiliate write-offs/diminution/ICD-arbitration cluster represents value and cash crystallising adversely through related parties. This is the forensic core of the Zee case — carried as the P3 finding — and is why the verdict is governance-driven rather than statement-driven.</div>
<div class="sub-title">Scoring (P5)</div>
<div class="prose"><p>Models clean-to-benign; unqualified audit opinion on the statements; clean CARO 2020 (no fraud by or on the Company); no restatement, going-concern or ICFR weakness; favourable (negative) accruals. Against this, two corroborated <strong>watch</strong> items — content-inventory NRV (auditor KAM) and the related-party value-leakage — hold the pillar at the top of the Low band rather than in the Clean band. Under §3.6 the pillar earns risk only from corroborated findings; there is no confirmed misstatement to take it to 5–6 or above.</p></div>
<div class="verdict-box"><div class="vscore"><div class="n" style="color:#3A4400">4</div><div class="d">/10</div></div><div class="vtext"><b>Low · 4.</b> The financial statements present a true and fair view: six benign models, unqualified opinion, clean CARO, no restatement, negative accruals. Two corroborated watch items (content NRV; related-party value-leakage) keep it at 4 rather than 2–3. The 4-vs-5 call is shown transparently — the case for Elevated (5) would weight the RPT value-leakage as a corroborated P5 earnings-quality finding rather than routing it to P3; held at 4 because the auditor took no exception and the statements are clean — flagged for analyst confirmation.</div></div>
