<div class="callout warn" style="margin-top:12px"><strong>Roll-up logic — the override binds.</strong> The composite is <strong>max(weighted blend, Pillar-5 floor, override floor)</strong>. The weighted blend (P5 60%, P6 12%, P4 10%, P1 8%, P2 5%, P3 5%) is <strong>≈5.6</strong> — dominated by the sound forensic pillar at 60%, which on its own would understate a governance-and-enforcement case. The Pillar-5 floor (4.0) is below that. The <strong>enforcement-tier override (F-2 analogue) fires at 7.0</strong> on the <strong>active SEBI-directed NSDL ISIN-level freeze plus the live SEBI fund-diversion proceeding</strong> — the India analogue of the §3.7 F-2 trigger (regulatory enforcement action / control concern). It binds, so the composite is <strong>7.0</strong>. <strong>F-1 (confirmed fraud, floor 9) does not fire:</strong> the SEBI fund-diversion matters are allegations under process, the 2023 interim bar was set aside by SAT, and the auditor reports no fraud — the framework scores allegation as risk, not as proof. Re-rate to F-1 only on an adjudicated SEBI/SFIO/NFRA fraud finding.</div>
<div class="note">The 60% Pillar-5 weight is intentional: it prevents a clean-board / clean-ownership profile from diluting a forensic finding. Here the inverse holds — a <em>sound</em> forensic core does not dilute a governance/enforcement finding, because the override floor operates independently of the blend.</div>
