<div class="card">
<div class="sub-title" style="margin-top:0"><span class="a-tag" id="s1">S1</span>Post-Period &amp; Supplementary Findings (FY26 documents)</div>
<div class="callout info"><strong>Provenance and status.</strong> The findings below are drawn from documents that post-date the FY2025 audited financials on which the body of this review is based: the FY26 quarterly earnings-call transcripts (Q1, Q3 and Q4 FY26), the FY26 Annual Secretarial Compliance Report (Vinod Kothari &amp; Co, Practising Company Secretaries, dated 28 May 2026, for the year ended 31 March 2026), and a Brickwork Ratings rationale dated 22 April 2022. They are recorded as analyst flags. The financial figures and pillar scores in the body are unchanged, and this addendum does not alter the composite of 7.0 — the enforcement-tier override already binds, and none of the new matter is an adjudicated fraud finding that would trigger the confirmed-fraud tier.</div>

<div class="sub-title">Legal &amp; Regulatory</div>
<ul class="ev-list">
<li class="f-high">SEBI fund-diversion show-cause notice independently corroborated. The FY26 secretarial compliance report examined SEBI (Prohibition of Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices) Regulations, 2003 specifically in relation to the show-cause notice issued by SEBI during the review period, confirming the SCN is live in FY26; the FY25 auditor likewise referenced SEBI enquiry/summons within the litigation KAM. Two independent sources thus support the override basis (P6).</li>
<li class="f-high">New — a concluded SEBI adjudication order dated 10 February 2026 against a promoter for violations of SEBI (SAST) Regulations: Reg 31(1) r/w 31(3) (creation of encumbrance), Reg 31(2) r/w 31(3) (invocation of pledge) and Reg 29(2) (transfer of shares). This is crystallised enforcement — a penalty order, distinct from the 2019 settlement already noted — and connects to the promoter stake collapse (P2): the holding fell to 3.99% via pledge invocations that carried the disclosure breaches now penalised.</li>
<li class="f-med">SCN respondent set is broader than the body states. Per the secretarial report, the SCN was issued against the listed entity, its promoters, the erstwhile managing director and certain subsidiaries — i.e. the company itself and group entities, not only the named individuals.</li>
<li class="f-med">Credit-rating gap. The latest credit-rating document on file is a Brickwork Ratings rationale (22 April 2022) that downgraded the issuer rating to BWR A-/Stable and simultaneously withdrew it at the company’s request, after a six-notch slide from BWR AAA (2019). No current public rating appears to be on record — a coverage gap the body omits. The 2022 downgrade was itself driven by uncertainties over the MCA Section 206(5) inspection, the same thread that runs into the present SEBI matters.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-title">Governance</div>
<ul class="ev-list">
<li class="f-med">Board composition (FY26 secretarial report): the board is constituted of Non-Executive and Independent Directors only. Punit Goenka was appointed CEO with effect from 18 November 2024 and is described as focused on operational responsibilities, with no Manager in the company — i.e. the founder-family CEO runs operations but does not sit on the board. Part mitigant (CEO separated from the board), part structural oddity (the CEO is not accountable as a director).</li>
<li class="f-pos">Confirmations from the secretarial report: no resignation of statutory auditors and no director disqualified under Section 164 during FY26 — both relevant to the P5 read (audit continuity is a methodology override trigger that did not fire) and to P1/P4.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-title">Accounting</div>
<ul class="ev-list">
<li class="f-high">Content-amortisation policy change (FY26) — the content-NRV watch crystallising. In Q4 FY26 the company moved movie amortisation from straight-line over 60 months to a front-loaded basis (50% in the first two years), taking a ~₹300 Cr charge. The FY25 policy (Note 2) confirms the prior straight-line / five-year basis, which deferred content cost relative to consumption and flattered FY23–FY25; the FY26 change is the correction. This converts the P5 content-NRV watch into a realised charge in the subsequent year.</li>
<li class="f-med">The FY26 secretarial compliance report provides no accounting assurance: it expressly states it has not verified the correctness of the financial records and is neither an audit nor an expression of opinion. It should not be cited as accounting comfort; the accounting flags remain sourced to the FY25 annual report and the FY26 transcript disclosure above.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-title">Related Party</div>
<ul class="ev-list">
<li class="f-high">RPT materiality policy updated late. The secretarial report flags that the Policy on materiality of and dealing with related-party transactions was updated beyond the time prescribed under Reg. 23(1) of the Listing Regulations — a governance deviation on the RPT framework itself — against the mitigant that prior Audit Committee approval was obtained for all RPTs in the period.</li>
<li class="f-med">Additional related-party impairment: Zee Learn Limited non-convertible debentures (₹25.5 Cr) are fully provided (100% diminution) — a further Essel-group value-leakage data point not in the body.</li>
<li class="f-med">Long-standing pattern corroborated. The 2022 Brickwork rationale documented related-party receivables from weak group entities (Dish TV) and a DSRA corporate guarantee to Siti Networks Ltd (₹200.9 Cr) whose default triggered the IndusInd insolvency petition (C.P.(IB)-221/2022) carried in P6 — i.e. the affiliate value-leakage pattern predates FY25 and traces one of the body’s litigation items to its origin.</li>
</ul>

<div class="sub-title">Confirmations — body watch-items reinforced</div>
<div class="callout pos"><strong>Where the FY26 documents confirm the review.</strong> Revenue-durability watch reinforced — FY26 nine-month advertising revenue down ~12% (vs −4.5% in FY25), with management guiding a similar full-year decline. Liquidity strength confirmed — cash and treasury investments of ₹2,760 Cr at March 2026, consistent with the near-debt-free balance sheet in the body. ZEE5 reached EBITDA breakeven in FY26, consistent with the OTT-migration narrative.</div>

